%description:

Whether a UE's results depend on its index.

They should not. Which slot of a module vector a UE occupies is a property of
how the network was written down, not of the radio environment, so swapping
two UEs' positions ought to swap their results and change nothing else. The
scenario is run twice, with the two UEs' positions exchanged, and every
recorded scalar of one run is compared against the correspondingly relabelled
scalar of the other.

The comparison is scoped to what a UE index can address: the UE's own channel
model, PHY, MAC and application results. The RLC entities are created on
demand and named after the MacNodeId, which itself follows module index, so
they move between parents when the UEs exchange cells; comparing those would
report a naming artifact as a physics difference.

Measured, rather than assumed. Switching the channel's randomness and the
interference computation on and off independently -- with dynamic LOS
disabled as well, so that "deterministic" means no draw of any kind -- gives:

    channel         interference     differing of 360 compared
    deterministic   off                 0
    deterministic   on                  0
    stochastic      off                18
    stochastic      on                 32

The configuration below keeps dynamic LOS on, which is the fuller exercise and
which is why the recorded verdict is 30 rather than 32: the LOS coin flip is
one more draw taken in evaluation order, and how many scalars two orderings
happen to agree on is not a meaningful quantity. The decomposition above is.

So the model is index-independent whenever the channel is deterministic --
including with interference enabled, which is worth stating because the
obvious suspect is the cross-cell state mutation in
computeDownlinkInterference, and on this evidence that is not by itself the
cause. What breaks the invariance is the stochastic state: the draws are
consumed from a shared stream in evaluation order, and evaluation order
follows module order, so exchanging two UEs hands each of them the other's
random numbers. The interference path makes it worse -- it draws more, in
cell-evaluation order -- but it is a contributor rather than the mechanism.

Recorded as an expected failure rather than fixed. Whether the model ought to
keep per-cell channel state or one shared per-link realization is a modelling
question -- the same one that decides a good part of the data layout of any
refactoring of the medium -- and it is not this test's to answer. What the
test is for is that the answer stops being a matter of belief: the deviation
is measured, named and visible, so nobody assumes an order-independence the
model does not have, and so the day it is fixed something says so.

%expected-failure: draws from a shared random stream are taken in module order, so results depend on UE index

%extraargs: -c ModuleOrderPermutation

%file: demo.xml
<config>
    <interface among='eNB* eNodeB* enb* *Enb* gNB* gNodeB* gnb* *Gnb* upf* *Upf* mecHost* pgw*' address='10.x.x.x' netmask='255.255.255.0'/>
    <interface hosts='*' address='192.168.x.x' netmask='255.255.255.0'/>
</config>

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[Config ModuleOrderPermutation]
network = simu5g.simulations.nr.networks.MultiCell_Standalone
sim-time-limit = 5s
**.routingRecorder.enabled = false
cmdenv-express-mode = true
cmdenv-status-frequency = 10000000s

output-scalar-file = results/${iterationvarsf}${repetition}.sca
output-vector-file = results/${iterationvarsf}${repetition}.vec
**.vector-recording = false

# As in GeometryInvariance: the runs are compared against each other, so they
# have to draw the same random numbers. seed-set follows the run number
# otherwise.
seed-set = 0

############### The permutation ##############
**.swapped = ${swap = 0, 1}

############### Geometry: two UEs, one per cell, positions exchanged ##############
*.gnb1.mobility.initialX = 250m
*.gnb1.mobility.initialY = 500m
*.gnb1.mobility.initialZ = 25m
*.gnb2.mobility.initialX = 750m
*.gnb2.mobility.initialY = 500m
*.gnb2.mobility.initialZ = 25m
*.numUe = 2
*.ue[*].servingNodeId = 0
*.ue[*].nrServingNodeId = 1
**.dynamicCellAssociation = true
**.enableHandover = false
*.ue[0].mobility.initialX = (${swap} == 0 ? 350m : 700m)
*.ue[0].mobility.initialY = (${swap} == 0 ? 450m : 560m)
*.ue[1].mobility.initialX = (${swap} == 0 ? 700m : 350m)
*.ue[1].mobility.initialY = (${swap} == 0 ? 560m : 450m)
*.ue[*].mobility.initialZ = 1.5m
**.mobility.constraintAreaMinX = 0m
**.mobility.constraintAreaMinY = 0m
**.mobility.constraintAreaMinZ = 0m
**.mobility.constraintAreaMaxX = 1500m
**.mobility.constraintAreaMaxY = 1000m
**.mobility.constraintAreaMaxZ = 50m
**.mobility.initFromDisplayString = false

############### The channel: interference on, state on ##############
**.scenario = "URBAN_MACROCELL"
**.nodebHeight = 25m
**.ueHeight = 1.5m
**.dynamicLos = true
**.fading = true
**.fadingType = "JAKES"
**.shadowing = true
**.downlinkInterference = true
**.uplinkInterference = true
**.bgCellInterference = false
**.extCellInterference = false

*.carrierAggregation.numComponentCarriers = 1
*.carrierAggregation.componentCarrier[0].carrierFrequency = 2GHz
*.carrierAggregation.componentCarrier[0].numBands = 6
**.numBands = 6

############### Traffic: identical for both UEs, so only position tells them apart ##############
*.ue[*].numApps = 1
*.server.numApps = 2
*.ue[*].app[0].typename = "CbrReceiver"
*.ue[*].app[0].localPort = 3000
*.server.app[*].typename = "CbrSender"
*.server.app[*].destAddress = "ue[" + string(ancestorIndex(0)) + "]"
*.server.app[*].destPort = 3000
*.server.app[*].localPort = 3088 + ancestorIndex(0)
*.server.app[*].packetSize = 100B
*.server.app[*].samplingTime = 0.01s
*.server.app[*].startTime = 0.1s

*.configurator.config = xmldoc("./demo.xml")

%postrun-command: ../../physics_reference.py --grade results >verdict.txt

%contains: verdict.txt
graded=1 failures=0

%not-contains: verdict.txt
FAILED
